  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 218 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Our next weather system arrives from the Great Lakes mid \nto late morning as a warm front draped across New York pushes east. \nIsentropic lift along the warm front will lead to widespread light \nto moderate stratiform rain on the leading edge of the system late \nthis morning through the afternoon, moving from the St. Lawrence \nValley eastward into central and northern Vermont. While the system \nwill be fairly progressive, projected precipitable water values in \nthe latest model runs reach as high as 1.75-2.25 inches. As rain \nturns more showery towards the later afternoon and evening today, \nheavy downpours will be possible. Much of central and northern \nVermont are currently in D0 (abnormally dry) to D1 (moderate \ndrought) levels, so we are not anticipating hydrological issues \nfrom this rainfall. Precipitation totals through early Thursday \nmorning will be highest across the on northern and western \nslopes of the mountains across our forecast area with around \n0.50-1.15 inches anticipated. Lower amounts are likely in the \nChamplain and Connecticut River valleys due to shadowing from \nthe mountains, closer to 0.20-0.60 inches in these areas. High \ntemperatures today will be seasonable in the lower and mid 70s, \nso elevated instability is favored over surface, and will likely\nbe competing with the preceding stable air. A few rumbles of \nthunder will be possible with modest overall instability and \nstrong 40-50 knots of shear. No severe or strong storms are \nexpected across northern New York or Vermont through the end of \nthe week. A strong low level jet of 40-50 kts at 850 mb will \nalso build in this evening, resulting in that shear mentioned. \nWhile most of these stronger winds aloft will not be realized at\nthe surface due to concurrent precipitation, gusts between \n25-35 MPH are likely, especially in the favored south-north \nchanneled valleys. For mariners, a Lake Wind Advisory is in \neffect for Lake Champlain today and tonight. Temperatures will \nremain mild overnight tonight in the upper 50s to mid 60s due to\nthe influx of warmer air in the warm sector of the system.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Widespread mild highs in the 70s are expected on \nThursday as gusty winds persist in favorable vertical mixing and \ndrying low levels behind the the system. Winds will likely gust \nbetween 20-30 MPH out of the west and northwest Thursday, then trend \nweaker into Friday but continue to be almost brisk in cold air \nadvection. This will result in a cooler day Friday with highs in the \nmid 60s to lower 70s. Depending on how light winds can become \nThursday night and Friday night, with high pressure nosing in from \nthe Great Lakes during this period, we can expect some patchy valley \nfog in the typical spots. Deep layer ridging will slide over the \nregion on Saturday, supporting a dry and sunny day after fog \ndissipates, with high temperatures back in the mid and upper 70s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: High pressure will build into the region towards the end \nof the week into the weekend, bringing a stretch of dry and pleasant \nweather. Daytime high temperatures on Saturday and Sunday will climb \nthe 70s to near 80 as the ridging shifts eastward leading to warm \nsoutherly flow across the region. While dry weather will persist \nthrough much of the weekend, precipitation chances will increase \nthrough the day on Sunday with a progressive shortwave trough \npushing through. There is still plenty of uncertainty regarding this \nfeature, with some uncertainty regarding how much moisture and \nwidespread shower activity will be, but currently it looks like the \nbetter chances will be towards the International Border. Another \nsystem looks to bring another round of widespread precipitation \nTuesday into Wednesday.",
